# Gradle incremental annotation processing registration.
#
# HkjHttpClientProcessor is ISOLATING. A client's native interface, facade and configuration are
# generated from that one interface, named as each file's only originating element, and from what
# it reaches: the interfaces it extends and its methods' types and annotations. Nothing it writes
# reads another client or the rest of the round. Gradle regenerates a client's files when it
# recompiles the client, so it never reprocesses an unchanged client from its class file, and the
# client's own binding names always come from source. A method inherited from a base Gradle did not
# recompile is read from that base's class file, and refused where that file records no parameter
# names, as one compiled without -parameters does. A hand-written class Gradle did not recompile is
# read from its class file too, and javac's Filer would write over it, so the processor refuses a
# name a class file without HKJ's @Generated already has. @HkjHttpClient stays SOURCE-retained,
# since an isolating processor is never handed a type by name.
org.higherkindedj.spring.client.processor.HkjHttpClientProcessor,isolating
# CompanionAnnotationProcessor generates nothing, only claims the hkj annotations the client
# processor reads or writes, so ISOLATING leaves Gradle nothing to track; AGGREGATING would reprocess
# every type carrying them, every generated client included.
org.higherkindedj.spring.client.processor.CompanionAnnotationProcessor,isolating
